# GitHub Actions

---

## What is GitHub Actions?

- A native CI/CD solution built into GitHub
- Automatically executes pipelines in response to GitHub events (push, pull request, issue, etc.)
- No separate server to install or maintain — GitHub manages the infrastructure
- Free for public/open-source repositories; usage limits apply for private repositories
- Comparable to GitLab CI (which is the equivalent for GitLab-hosted repositories)

---

## When to Use GitHub Actions vs Jenkins

| Use GitHub Actions if… | Use Jenkins if… |
|------------------------|-----------------|
| All code lives on GitHub and will stay there | Code may migrate to GitLab, Azure DevOps, AWS CodeCommit, etc. |
| Minimal maintenance overhead is a priority | Full plugin ecosystem and complex orchestration are needed |
| Project is open source / public | Organization requires self-hosted, air-gapped CI infrastructure |
| Team wants simple YAML-based pipelines | Fine-grained control over build agents is required |

> **Key rule:** GitHub Actions is platform-locked to GitHub. If the organization might move to a different VCS in the future, prefer a platform-agnostic tool like Jenkins.

---

## Core Concepts

| Concept | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| **Workflow** | A YAML file that defines one automated process (equivalent to a Jenkins pipeline) |
| **Event / Trigger** | The GitHub activity that starts a workflow (e.g. `push`, `pull_request`, `issues`) |
| **Job** | A group of steps that run on the same runner (equivalent to a Jenkins pipeline within a file) |
| **Step** | A single task inside a job — either a shell command or a pre-built Action |
| **Action** | A reusable plugin from the GitHub Marketplace (e.g. `actions/checkout`, `actions/setup-python`) |
| **Runner** | The server/container that executes the job (GitHub-hosted or self-hosted) |

---

## File Structure

All workflow files must be placed in this exact directory at the root of the repository:

```
<repo-root>/
└── .github/
    └── workflows/
        ├── ci.yaml          # e.g. build and test
        ├── deploy.yaml      # e.g. deploy to Kubernetes
        └── pr-check.yaml    # e.g. validate PR title/description
```

- Multiple workflow files can exist in the `workflows/` folder
- Each file can have its own trigger and runs independently
- GitHub executes all matching workflows when a trigger event fires

---

## Anatomy of a Workflow File

```yaml
name: My First GitHub Actions Workflow   # display name in GitHub UI

on: [push, pull_request]                 # trigger events

jobs:
  build:                                 # job ID (any name)
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest               # runner image

    steps:
- name: Checkout code
        uses: actions/checkout@v3        # pre-built Action (plugin)

- name: Set up environment
        uses: actions/setup-python@v2    # another pre-built Action
        with:
          python-version: '3.9'

- name: Install dependencies
        run: pip install pytest           # shell command

- name: Run tests
        run: pytest                       # shell command
```

Key syntax notes:
- **`on:`** — one or more trigger events; GitHub starts the workflow when any of them fires
- **`runs-on:`** — the container image for the runner (`ubuntu-latest`, `windows-latest`, `macos-latest`)
- **`uses:`** — references a pre-built Action from the Marketplace; `@v3` is the Action's version (not the tool version)
- **`with:`** — passes parameters to an Action
- **`run:`** — executes a raw shell command

---

## Example 1 — Python Unit Test Pipeline

### Source files

```python
# src/addition.py
def add(a, b):
    return a + b
```

```python
# src/test_addition.py
from addition import add

def test_add():
    assert add(2, 3) == 5
```

### Workflow file

```yaml
# .github/workflows/ci.yaml
name: Python CI

on: [push]

jobs:
  test:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest

    strategy:
      matrix:
        python-version: ['3.8', '3.9']   # run on both versions

    steps:
- name: Checkout repository
        uses: actions/checkout@v3

- name: Set up Python ${{ matrix.python-version }}
        uses: actions/setup-python@v2
        with:
          python-version: ${{ matrix.python-version }}

- name: Install dependencies
        run: |
          pip install pytest

- name: Run unit tests
        run: pytest src/
```

When this workflow runs with two Python versions defined in the matrix, GitHub Actions creates **two parallel jobs** — one for Python 3.8 and one for Python 3.9.

---

## Example 2 — Java Build with Maven, SonarQube & Kubernetes Deploy

```yaml
# .github/workflows/deploy.yaml
name: Java CI/CD

on: [push]

jobs:
  build-and-deploy:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest

    steps:
- name: Checkout code
        uses: actions/checkout@v3

- name: Set up Java
        uses: actions/setup-java@v3
        with:
          java-version: '17'
          distribution: 'temurin'

- name: Build with Maven
        run: mvn clean install

- name: SonarQube scan
        env:
          SONAR_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.SONAR_TOKEN }}
        run: mvn sonar:sonar -Dsonar.login=$SONAR_TOKEN

- name: Set up kubectl
        uses: azure/setup-kubectl@v3

- name: Deploy to Kubernetes
        env:
          KUBECONFIG_DATA: ${{ secrets.KUBECONFIG }}
        run: |
          echo "$KUBECONFIG_DATA" | base64 --decode > kubeconfig.yaml
          kubectl apply -f deployment/deployment.yaml --kubeconfig=kubeconfig.yaml
```

> Sensitive values (`SONAR_TOKEN`, `KUBECONFIG`) are stored as GitHub Secrets and accessed via `${{ secrets.SECRET_NAME }}` — they are never exposed in logs.

---

## Plugins / Actions Marketplace

GitHub Actions has a marketplace of reusable Actions (equivalent to Jenkins plugins), but with zero installation steps.

| Task | Action to use |
|------|--------------|
| Checkout code | `actions/checkout@v3` |
| Set up Python | `actions/setup-python@v2` |
| Set up Java | `actions/setup-java@v3` |
| Set up Node.js | `actions/setup-node@v3` |
| Set up Ruby | `actions/setup-ruby@v1` |
| Set up kubectl | `azure/setup-kubectl@v3` |
| Cache dependencies | `actions/cache@v3` |
| Upload build artifact | `actions/upload-artifact@v3` |

**How to discover Actions:**
1. Go to [github.com/marketplace?type=actions](https://github.com/marketplace?type=actions)
2. Or browse the [GitHub Actions documentation](https://docs.github.com/en/actions) → Quickstart → choose your language/framework

> The `@v3` in `actions/checkout@v3` refers to the **version of the Action plugin**, not the version of the tool being set up.

---

## Matrix Builds — Testing Across Multiple Versions

A matrix strategy runs the same job across multiple combinations of variables, creating parallel jobs automatically.

```yaml
strategy:
  matrix:
    python-version: ['3.8', '3.9', '3.10']
    os: [ubuntu-latest, windows-latest]
```

This creates **6 parallel jobs** (3 Python versions × 2 operating systems).

To test on a single version only, simplify to:

```yaml
steps:
- uses: actions/setup-python@v2
    with:
      python-version: '3.9'
```

---

## Storing Secrets

Secrets (passwords, tokens, kubeconfig files, API keys) must never be hard-coded in workflow files.

**How to add a secret:**
1. Go to the GitHub repository → **Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions**
2. Click **New repository secret**
3. Enter a name (e.g. `SONAR_TOKEN`) and value
4. Click **Add secret**

**How to use a secret in a workflow:**

```yaml
env:
  MY_SECRET: ${{ secrets.MY_SECRET_NAME }}
```

Or inline:

```yaml
run: kubectl apply -f deploy.yaml --token=${{ secrets.K8S_TOKEN }}
```

> Secret values are masked in all workflow logs — they never appear in plain text.

---

## Self-Hosted Runners

By default, GitHub provides hosted runners (`ubuntu-latest`, etc.) with limited compute. For workloads requiring more CPU/RAM (e.g. load testing, large builds), a self-hosted runner can be configured.

**When to use self-hosted runners:**
- GitHub-hosted runners don't have enough compute resources
- Security/compliance requirements mandate running builds on internal infrastructure
- Need access to private network resources

**How to set up:**
1. Go to **Settings → Actions → Runners → New self-hosted runner**
2. Select OS and follow the installation script GitHub provides
3. The runner registers itself and appears in the runner list
4. In the workflow, specify:

```yaml
runs-on: self-hosted
```

---

## GitHub Actions vs Jenkins — Comparison

| Feature | GitHub Actions | Jenkins |
|---------|---------------|---------|
| **Hosting** | Managed by GitHub | Self-hosted EC2 / server required |
| **Setup time** | Zero (just create a YAML file) | Install Java, Jenkins, plugins, configure agents |
| **Maintenance** | None | OS updates, plugin updates, security patches |
| **Cost (public repos)** | Free | Cost of EC2 instance + storage |
| **Cost (private repos)** | ~2,000 free minutes/month; paid beyond that | Cost of infrastructure only |
| **Plugin ecosystem** | Growing; newer and more limited | Mature; thousands of plugins |
| **Platform lock-in** | Locked to GitHub | Works with any VCS |
| **Secrets management** | Native (built-in) | Credentials plugin required |
| **Matrix builds** | Native (built-in) | Requires additional configuration |
| **UI complexity** | Simple GitHub UI | Separate Jenkins UI to manage |
| **Pipeline as code** | ✅ YAML in `.github/workflows/` | ✅ Jenkinsfile in repo |
| **Best for** | Open source, GitHub-only projects | Multi-platform, enterprise, complex pipelines |

